  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAfter a cool start /mid and upper 30s in mountain valleys/,\nstrong warming once we break the inversion with values climbing\nwell into the 50s and lower 60s by lunch. With a dry atmosphere\nin place /denoted by PWATS around 0.40\"/ coordinated with \nadjacent offices and lowered dewpoints several degrees this\nafternoon. Looking at observations yesterday, we bottomed out \nin the mid and upper 30s, so kept them just a touch above that. \n\nThere is a low chance we will need another frost advisory for \nFriday night into Saturday morning for eastern Tucker. \nProbabilities of temps below 36F are less than 10%. NBM 5th \npercentile still has values in the upper 30s. That said, Canaan\nValley and frost pockets nearby have been observed at that \npercentile, so we will see what they get to this morning and \nmake a decision later today. We also have to keep in mind that\narea is a micro-climate. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsemble clusters are in great agreement through Day 5 /Sept\n29-30th/. A mid level anticyclone continues to dominate our \nweather. The column warms each day, especially after Sunday \nwhen temps get back above normal and remain there the balance of\nthe week. We will see an increase in moisture Sunday especially\nwith mid and high clouds from the nor'easter. This will affect \nwestern PA and northern WV mainly. Eastern Ohio will be far \nenough west to continue to bask in a mostly sunny sky. With the\nincreased cloud cover, daytimes highs pull back a few degrees \nSunday in the aforementioned locations when compared to \nSaturday. \n\nDon't stick a fork in summer weather yet all you fall\nweather fans. A strong mid level anticyclone reaffirms itself \nover the Ohio Valley Thursday through next weekend. With H5 \nheights pushing 590dm from the GEFS and EPS. Daytime highs \noutside of the mountains could climb back into the lower 80s by \nDay 7. There is a 30-50% across southwestern PA and northern WV \nof eclipsing 80F.\n\nThe only time frame when precipitation is mentioned in the\nseven day forecast is Wednesday when the ridge breaks down \nallowing a shortwave trough passage. As of now, pops remain in \nthe low chance category. Based on the lack of appreciable \nforcing with this feature and a relatively dry atmosphere, any \nprecip should be less than a tenth of an inch in a 12 hour \nperiod. Stats for 12 hours QPF exceeding 0.10\" reside in the \n15-20% range.",
